Thanks, Chris. The original raster layer for my study area has NA values that are a Landsat artifact and I hadn't realized the degree to which some of those scan lines intersect with several of the sessions in the ssDF. Will have to think about the best way to deal with that.

Thank you so much! I had been going a bit nuts trying to figure out the problem and really appreciate the help.

On Sunday, January 24, 2021 at 7:10:29 PM UTC-5 Chris Sutherland wrote:
> It's worth considering how the NAs came to be. The na.rm call tells the raster function extract to ignore NAs when summarizing values within a buffer of a point. If all values within a buffer are NAs then it will return an NA. Do you think this is possible? This can happen when the spatial extent of the state space doesn't match the rest (specifically, it's bigger).
>
> The simplest approach would be to simply remove any rows in the state space that have an NAs.
>
